# 3. The "SysAdmin" Challenge: Keeping the Wallet Balanced
# Unlike a bank account, your wallet doesn't have a digital statement. It is very easy to forget a $20 purchase. To keep it accurate, you should perform a "Cash Count" once a week.
# 
# If you count your wallet and find you have $450, but hledger thinks you have $480, you can add an adjustment to account for "forgotten" small spends:
# 
# Plaintext
# 2026-04-18 Cash Adjustment | Lost or forgotten
#     expenses:misc:adjustments       $30.00
#     assets:cash:wallet              $450.00  ; This "asserts" the total is now 450
# 4. Moving Money: Bank to Wallet
# When you go to an ATM to get cash, you aren't spending money; you are just moving it between assets.
# 
# Plaintext
# 2026-04-20 ATM | Withdrawal
#     assets:cash:wallet              $1000.00
#     assets:bank:checking


# --- SiVale Benefit Cards ---
account assets:sivale:pantry
account assets:sivale:meals
account assets:sivale:services

# --- Related Income ---
account income:benefits:sivale

# assets:sivale:pantry (Despensa - for groceries, usually accepted at supermarkets)
# assets:sivale:meals (Restaurante - for prepared food/restaurants)
# assets:sivale:services (Servicios - for utilities like light, water, or gas)

# 2. How to Record the "Income" (The Deposit)
# When your employer deposits money into these cards, it isn't "Bank" money, but it is still income. You should record the movement from an income account to the specific card.
# Plaintext
# 2026-04-15 Schneider Electric | Pantry Deposit
#     assets:sivale:grocery             $2,500.00
#     income:benefits:sivale
# 3. How to Record Spending
# When you use the cards, the money moves from the SiVale asset to the corresponding expense.
# Using the Meal Card:
# Plaintext
# 2026-04-16 Chedraui | Groceries
#     expenses:food:groceries           $850.00
#     assets:sivale:grocery
# Using the Services Card (e.g., for Light or Water):
# Plaintext
# 2026-04-17 CFE | Electricity - San Pedro
#     expenses:housing:utilities:electricity:san-pedro    $400.00
#     assets:sivale:services
# 4. Setting up Aliases for Speed
# Since you'll likely use these cards often, add these aliases to your accounts.journal:
# Plaintext
# alias siv-meal = assets:sivale:grocery
# alias siv-serv = assets:sivale:services
# Now, in your 2026.journal, you can just type:
# Plaintext
# 2026-04-18 OXXO | Gas for Stove
#     expenses:housing:utilities:gas    $300.00
#     siv-serv
# 5. Why track them separately?
# As a "SysAdmin" of your own life, tracking these separately allows you to run a very specific report:
# hledger bal assets:sivale
# This will show you exactly how much "hidden" money you have left for food versus how much you have left for bills like Tenencia or Predial (if your card allows those payments).
# Pro-Tip: The "Master" View
# If you want to see all your liquid money (Bank + Wallet + SiVale), you can run:
# hledger bal assets
# This gives you a true "Total Assets" view, which is more accurate than just checking your bank app.

#account assets:transfers:wife
#account expenses:family:transfer
# difference between assets:transfers:wife and expenses:family:transfer
# The difference comes down to one question: Do you ever expect that money to come back, or is it gone for good?
# In plain-text accounting, choosing between an Asset and an Expense changes how that money affects your net worth on your balance sheet.
# 1. expenses:family:transfer (Money Spent)
# When you use an Expense account, you are telling hledger: "This money has left my ecosystem. It is gone, and I am not tracking what happens to it next."
# 	- How it acts: It immediately reduces your net worth. It shows up on your Income Statement (hledger is) as money spent this month.
# 	- When to use it: If you and your wife run completely separate finances. Once you give her the money, it belongs to her personal budget, and you don't expect her to pay you back or give you a receipt for how she spent it.
# 2. assets:transfers:wife (Money Shifted)
# When you use an Asset account, you are telling hledger: "This money moved out of my bank account, but it is still 'my' value or family value. It is just sitting in a different place right now."
# 	- How it acts: It does not change your net worth. It won't show up as an expense. Instead, it stays on your Balance Sheet (hledger bs) under your assets.
# 	- When to use it: You usually use this as a clearing account or a temporary holding spot. For example:
# 		- You give her $500 to buy a new household appliance next week. It's an asset right now because the money hasn't been spent yet. Once she buys it and shows you the receipt, you clear it out:
# 			2026-07-01 Bought Fridge
# 				expenses:appliances          $500.00
# 				assets:transfers:wife       -$500.00
# 		- Or, you treat it like an informal loan/tab that you expect to balance out later if she transfers money back to you.
# Summary Checklist
# If the money is...													Use Account					hledger Impact
# An allowance, a gift, or just her share of separate finances		expenses:family:transfer	Reduces your net worth today.
# For a future purchase she will make later, or an informal "tab"		assets:transfers:wife		Net worth stays the same until the final receipt is booked.

# Go with expenses:family:transfer:wife_name (replacing wife_name with her actual name).
# Here is why adding her name is the better move for your journal:
# 1. Future Flexibility (The "Just in Case" Rule)
# Right now, you are only transferring money to her. But down the road, you might need to track money transfers to your kids, your parents, or other family needs. If everything is dumped into expenses:family:transfer, it becomes a headache to untangle later.
# 2. Cleaner Reporting
# hledger handles nested accounts beautifully. If you use the specific name, you get the best of both worlds:
# 	- If you run hledger balance expenses:family, it will automatically roll up the totals and show you everything spent on the family as a whole.
# 	- If you run hledger balance expenses:family:transfer, it will break down exactly how much went to her versus anyone else.
account expenses:family:transfer:carmen